## Correctness and recovery

Each actor writes a unique staging run; manifests become immutable only after every referenced artifact is durable and hashed. No manifest means no usable run. Concurrent workers never share writable artifacts (separate-before-serializing-shared-state).

Commit takes the existing publication lock and checks the expected base generation. One transaction imports the complete projection, reconciles explicitly removed source keys, records artifact fingerprints, advances coverage, and switches the evidence pointer. A stale base requires replan/merge against the new base, never last-writer-wins. Crash before commit leaves reusable staged evidence; crash after commit returns `AlreadyPublished`. Artifact retention must pin every committed generation; database rollback cannot restore deleted files.

Full global resolution preserves cross-member aliases, company names, parent dependencies, and merging/splitting evidence. Unchanged children are reconsidered when parents change. Noncompany identity IDs remain generation-scoped: **stable external identity IDs are a separate requirement**, not solved by this acquisition design. Do not publish only changed declaration rows after resolving a new complete generation.

Periodic reconciliation scans the full cohort, compares evidence, and uses the same publication protocol. Existing loader preservation of omitted declarations must change for authoritative removals. Until deletion semantics are established, omissions become retained evidence with an explicit discrepancy report, not automatic deletion. Coverage promises successful query traversal, not proof that upstream history cannot change retrospectively.

## Tradeoffs accepted

- Accept full member downloads for changed members in exchange for complete parent context and fewer filter/version assumptions.
- Accept global compute and complete generation publication in exchange for preserving current resolution semantics.
- Accept overlap and periodic reconciliation in exchange for operating without a documented cursor or snapshot.

## Alternatives considered

Full acquisition plus content diff hides similar replay complexity but saves no acquisition work; useful fallback. Direct interest deltas merged into snapshots use less bandwidth but need verified version completeness and parent recovery. Fully incremental resolution requires durable identity evidence, merge/split policy, reverse dependency closure, and historical alias reconciliation; its public operations conceal a substantially larger new correctness subsystem.

